# Earthquake Pulse — Real-Time Seismic Analytics with Airflow

An end-to-end, production-style data engineering project built on **Apache
Airflow**, ingesting **live global earthquake data** from the USGS public
API, processing it through a **Bronze → Silver → Gold Medallion
Architecture**, and landing near-real-time, BI-ready KPIs in PostgreSQL.

This is a same-architecture variation of a flight-tracking Airflow
project: identical orchestration pattern (idempotent, time-windowed,
retry-safe, UPSERT-based Medallion pipeline) applied to a different public
data source. Where that project asked "how is global air traffic
behaving right now?", this one asks the same question of the planet
itself: **how is global seismic activity behaving right now?**

> Not a DAG-syntax tutorial. This project is built the way a production
> pipeline is actually built: idempotent tasks, retries, incremental
> time-windowed extraction, UPSERT-based analytics tables, and a data
> quality gate before anything reaches a dashboard.

---

## 🧠 Project Use Case

This project simulates how a seismic monitoring / earth-sciences team
tracks global earthquake activity **patterns** — not individual quakes —
to:

- Monitor global seismic event volume trends over time
- Detect **swarm signals** — a region's activity spiking well above its
  own trailing baseline (the seismic analogue of "congestion detection")
- Compare activity across regions and countries, window over window
- Build a historical, analytics-ready dataset from a live feed, ready for
  Power BI / Metabase / Superset / any BI tool that speaks Postgres

