## 🏗️ Architecture

```
USGS FDSNWS API  ──▶  Bronze (raw JSON)  ──▶  Silver (clean, typed)  ──▶  Gold (BI-ready KPIs)
  (public, no key)      1 row / event          1 row / event,             4 aggregate tables,
                         full raw payload       deduped + normalized       UPSERT per run window
                         preserved in JSONB      on event_id
```

**Orchestrator:** Apache Airflow 2.9 (LocalExecutor)
**Storage:** PostgreSQL 16 (separate `airflow_meta` DB for Airflow itself,
`earthquake_analytics` DB for bronze/silver/gold)
**Source:** [USGS Earthquake FDSNWS API](https://earthquake.usgs.gov/fdsnws/event/1/) — public, free, no API key required
**Schedule:** hourly, using Airflow's `data_interval_start` /
`data_interval_end` as the exact `[start, end)` query window

### Why the FDSNWS `query` endpoint, not the rolling summary feeds

USGS also publishes rolling feeds like `all_hour.geojson` — but those only
ever answer "what happened in the last N", which makes a reproducible
backfill impossible. The FDSNWS `query` endpoint accepts explicit
`starttime`/`endtime`, so every DAG run — including a manual backfill —
requests an exact, reproducible slice of the catalog. Re-running the same
data interval returns the same events (modulo USGS revisions, handled via
`updated` timestamps — see below). That's the idempotency property a real
pipeline needs and a rolling feed can't give you.

### Medallion layers

| Layer | Table(s) | Grain | What happens here |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Bronze** | `bronze.earthquakes_raw` | 1 row per raw GeoJSON feature | Raw API payload landed byte-for-byte in a `JSONB` column. Nothing is reshaped. `ON CONFLICT (batch_id, event_id) DO NOTHING` makes retries safe. |
| **Silver** | `silver.earthquakes_clean` | 1 row per unique `event_id` | Parses/types every field, normalizes the (genuinely messy) `place` string into `region` + `country`, dedupes. UPSERTed with an `updated_time` guard so a stale reprocess can never overwrite a newer USGS revision. |
| **Gold** | `gold.region_activity_window`, `gold.global_summary_window`, `gold.swarm_signals`, `gold.daily_region_summary` | Per run window / per day | Pure SQL aggregation, UPSERTed per run window — safe to recompute, never double-counts. |

### The one deliberately messy part: `place` parsing

USGS's free-text `place` field is inconsistent by design (the same way
flight data forces you to parse messy route/airport strings):

```
"10km SSW of Ridgecrest, CA"            -> region="Ridgecrest, CA",        country="United States"
"35 km NNW of Sutton-Alpine, Alaska"    -> region="Sutton-Alpine, Alaska", country="United States"
"88 km SSE of Kimbe, Papua New Guinea"  -> region="Kimbe, Papua New Guinea", country="Papua New Guinea"
"Fiji region"                           -> region="Fiji",                 country="Fiji"
"northern Mid-Atlantic Ridge"           -> region="northern Mid-Atlantic Ridge", country="International Waters / Unclassified"
```

`include/transform.py::parse_place` handles this with explicit heuristics
(full US state names *and* abbreviations, oceanic-feature detection,
territory handling) — and it was validated against a **live pull of the
current day's actual USGS feed**, not just synthetic examples, during
development. See `tests/test_transform.py` for the regression cases that
came out of that validation (e.g. `"Texas"` vs `"CA"` — USGS spells some
states out and abbreviates others).

