### Run it

```bash
source scripts/local_dev_env.sh   # the WSL equivalent of `.env` — points Airflow at this project + Postgres
airflow db init                   # the WSL equivalent of the "airflow-init" container
airflow standalone                # the WSL equivalent of `docker compose up -d` — webserver + scheduler + triggerer, one process
```

`airflow standalone` prints an admin username/password to the terminal on
its first run — use that to log in. Every terminal after this first one
just needs `source venv/bin/activate && source scripts/local_dev_env.sh`
before running any `airflow ...` command — `airflow db init` is one-time
only.

Airflow UI: [http://localhost:8080](http://localhost:8080)

The DAG `earthquake_medallion_pipeline` is scheduled `@hourly` and starts
paused — unpause it in the UI, then hit **Trigger DAG** for an immediate
first run rather than waiting for the schedule. Before that first run,
finish the two UI steps in "Fully UI-driven local setup" below (adding
the `analytics_db` connection and the two tuning Variables) — without
those the DAG will fail on its first task.

### Connect a BI tool

Point Power BI / Metabase / Superset / DBeaver at:

```
host:     localhost
port:     5432
database: earthquake_analytics
schema:   gold
user/pw:  analytics / analytics   (or whatever you used above)
```

### Backfill historical data

```bash
airflow dags backfill earthquake_medallion_pipeline \
  --start-date 2026-08-01 --end-date 2026-08-07
```

Every backfilled hour lands correctly and idempotently — running it twice
does not duplicate rows anywhere in bronze, silver, or gold.

### Prefer Docker instead?

The project still ships a working `docker-compose.yml` / `Dockerfile` /
`.env.example` for that path:

```bash
cp .env.example .env
docker compose up -d
```

Same DAG, same Postgres schema, same UI at `localhost:8080` — pick
whichever fits your machine. Nothing below this point differs between
the two.

### Run the unit tests (no Docker required)

```bash
python3 -m pip install pytest requests
python3 -m pytest tests/ -v
```

### Test ingestion in isolation, no Airflow/DB needed

```bash
python3 scripts/run_ingestion_standalone.py --hours-ago 2
```

Fetches a live window straight from USGS and prints what each event
normalizes to. Useful as a 5-second pre-flight check before a demo, or
while iterating on `include/transform.py`.

