## 🖱️ Fully UI-driven local setup (no Docker)

This runs everything through the Airflow UI wherever Airflow actually
allows it. One honest caveat up front: Airflow's **own** metadata database
and executor have to be set *before* the UI exists (the UI is served
*by* Airflow, so it can't configure the thing that starts it) — that
one piece is a few environment variables via `scripts/local_dev_env.sh`.
Everything downstream of that — the pipeline's own database connection,
its tuning knobs, triggering runs, reading logs, retrying tasks — is
point-and-click from the browser.

**1 & 2. Install Airflow and start it** — this is exactly the "Getting
started (WSL, no Docker)" section above: install Postgres + Airflow once,
then `source scripts/local_dev_env.sh && airflow db init && airflow
standalone`. Open [http://localhost:8080](http://localhost:8080) and log
in with the admin credentials `airflow standalone` printed on first run.

**3. Add the database connection — Admin > Connections > +**

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Connection Id | `analytics_db` |
| Connection Type | `Postgres` |
| Host | `localhost` |
| Schema | `earthquake_analytics` |
| Login | `analytics` |
| Password | `analytics` |
| Port | `5432` |

This is exactly the connection `include/db.py` looks up by id — nothing
in the DAG or Python code needs to change.

**4. Add the two tuning Variables — Admin > Variables > +**

| Key | Value |
|---|---|
| `usgs_min_magnitude` | `0` |
| `swarm_spike_ratio` | `2.0` |

**5. Run it from the DAG list**

Find `earthquake_medallion_pipeline`, flip its pause toggle off, then hit
the ▶ **Trigger DAG** button for an immediate run instead of waiting for
the hourly schedule. Click into the run's **Grid** view — each square is
a task; click any square to open its log panel right there in the
browser (no `docker logs` / SSH needed). A failed task can be retried,
or cleared, or marked success, all from that same panel.

One real limitation worth knowing: a true historical **backfill**
(`airflow dags backfill --start-date ... --end-date ...`) is CLI-only in
Airflow 2.9 — there's no UI button for it. For a demo this doesn't matter
(you're triggering forward-looking runs), it only matters if you later
want to reprocess a week of history.

