## 🧩 Extending this project

- **BigQuery as a future upgrade**: `sql_bigquery/*.sql` and
  `include/bq_client.py` sketch out what swapping the analytics warehouse
  from Postgres to BigQuery looks like — three datasets (bronze/silver/gold)
  instead of three schemas, `MERGE` statements instead of `INSERT ... ON
  CONFLICT` (BigQuery has no such syntax), partitioning/clustering instead
  of indexes. It's intentionally **not** wired into a DAG yet: BigQuery has
  no local emulator, so unlike everything else in this project, that code
  couldn't be proven against a live database before being handed to you —
  and getting it demo-ready needs real GCP setup time (service account,
  IAM, a live project) that a first walkthrough shouldn't be gated on. Once
  you've got the Postgres version running end to end and want to take this
  on, ask and we can wire it into a second DAG and validate it against your
  actual BigQuery project.
- **Swap Postgres for a lakehouse layout**: land Bronze as partitioned
  Parquet in S3/MinIO instead of a JSONB table, keep Silver/Gold in
  Postgres (or DuckDB) — same DAG shape, different storage adapter in
  `include/db.py`.
- **dbt for the Silver → Gold layer**: the SQL in
  `include/gold_aggregations.py` is already written as plain, testable SQL
  — a natural next step is moving it into a dbt project and having Airflow
  trigger `dbt run` instead of raw psycopg2 calls.
- **Alerting**: wire `gold.swarm_signals` rows with `signal_level =
  'swarm'` to a Slack/PagerDuty task via Airflow's callback hooks —
  same shape as alerting on a flight-congestion signal.
- **Great Expectations**: replace the hand-rolled `data_quality_check`
  task with a Great Expectations suite for richer, declarative DQ rules.

